      <!-- The encoding description shared by all advertisements. Each
           advertisement carries the project description, the editorial
           principles and those renditions and topics that it uses. After
           changing this file, run `npm run sync-tei` in frontend/. -->
      <encodingDesc>
         <projectDesc>
            <p>The Welte-Mignon Advertisements Collection brings together print
               advertisements for the Welte-Mignon reproducing piano that appeared
               between 1905 and 1930. It was created as part of the PhD project
               "Grünfelds Geist" at the University of Tübingen, which considers
               piano rolls as objects of a digital edition. The collection is
               published at https://anz.welte225.org.</p>
         </projectDesc>
         <samplingDecl>
            <p>The collection gathers print advertisements for the Welte-Mignon
               reproducing piano that appeared between 1905 and 1930 and were
               published by M. Welte &amp; Söhne, by one of its licensees, or by a
               dealer or agent selling the instrument. Each advertisement is
               encoded once; its further printings, in the same or in other
               periodicals, are listed as further sources.</p>
            <p>The advertisements were found in digitised newspapers and
               magazines: Chronicling America (Library of Congress), the British
               Newspaper Archive, HathiTrust (copies of the Universities of
               California, Illinois, Michigan, Minnesota and Virginia), the
               Internet Archive, the Digitale Sammlungen of the Bayerische
               Staatsbibliothek, ANNO (Austrian National Library), the
               International Arcade Museum Library and Georgia Historic
               Newspapers.</p>
         </samplingDecl>
         <editorialDecl>
            <p>Each file transcribes one printing of an advertisement, the one
               whose scan its facsimile zones refer to; <att>decls</att> on
               <gi>text</gi> points to its <gi>sourceDesc</gi>. The further
               printings were identified as the same advertisement but not
               collated word for word; differences that were noticed are
               recorded with <gi>app</gi> or in a note.</p>
            <p>The regions and lines of the scans were mostly marked in
               eScriptorium and converted from PAGE XML to TEI. The text was in
               part recognised automatically (HTR) and corrected against the
               scan, in part transcribed by hand.</p>
            <correction>
               <p>Errors of the original are not corrected but marked with
                  <gi>sic</gi>.</p>
            </correction>
            <normalization>
               <p>Spelling and punctuation follow the original and are not
                  normalised. Text set in capitals is often transcribed in mixed
                  case and marked with the rendition <val>#uc</val>.</p>
            </normalization>
            <hyphenation eol="all">
               <p>Hyphens at the end of a line are kept as printed.</p>
            </hyphenation>
            <segmentation>
               <p>Every region of the original page is a <gi>div</gi> that points to
                  its zone in the <gi>facsimile</gi> with <att>facs</att>. The
                  lines of the original are marked with <gi>lb</gi>; the typography
                  is recorded with <att>rendition</att>.</p>
            </segmentation>
            <interpretation>
               <p>Parts of an advertisement that concern other products than the
                  Welte-Mignon are not transcribed but marked with <gi>gap</gi>,
                  which describes them in a <gi>desc</gi>. Illustrations are
                  described in <gi>figure</gi>. Persons are linked to the GND with
                  <att>corresp</att>. Recurring motifs are marked as topics with
                  <att>ana</att> on <gi>seg</gi> and <gi>figure</gi>, pointing to
                  the taxonomy of topics; these are the editor's interpretation
                  (<att>resp</att>).</p>
            </interpretation>
            <stdVals>
               <p>Dates of publication are given in ISO 8601 in <att>when</att>,
                  as precisely as they are known.</p>
            </stdVals>
         </editorialDecl>
